    \u0438\u0434\u0443\u0442 \u0440\u0443\u043a\u0430 \u043e\u0431 \u0440\u0443\u043a\u0443
    \u0441 \u0447\u0435\u043b\u043e\u0432\u0435\u0447\u0435\u0441\u043a\u0438\u043c\u0438 \u0441\u043b\u0430\u0431\u043e\u0441\u0442\u044f\u043c\u0438."
  interestingFactsEn: "William Rowan Hamilton's personality is so multifaceted that behind the dry lines of scientific work, there are lots of chickens, paradoxes, and amazing stories hidden behind him. He was both the most \"unartificial\" astronomer in history and the most \"earliest\" genius who cut out the formulas on stone, played children in arithmetic, and argued with his wife over flies. This is a collection of the most interesting, interesting and sometimes incredible facts of his life — as detailed as possible.--- 1.\n\n\"Vundercind Poliglo: 13 years and 13 languages of Hamilton are often compared to other genius, but as a child, he has outdone even Michel Nostradamus and John Stewart Mill in linguistics, raised by an uncle-vicar, a boy who grew up in an atmosphere where language skills were considered a sign of aristocraticism.However, by the age of 13, William knew not the standard set (Latin, Greek, French, Italian) but exotic dead and living languages: Hebrew, Syrian, Khaldey (Arabic), Arabic, Persian, Sanskrit, Bengali, Malay, and even Marathi.His uncle's diary records that by the age of 10, William could read morning prayers in eight different languages in turn, and he was not just a botrail -- he compared the seven-ton roots to the Indo-Europeans, trying to find a common prancing language, that is, he did linguistic comparatives at the amateur level before it became an academic discipline -- 2.\n\nThe loss that changed life in 1818, when William was 13 years old, came to Dublin with the tour of Zerah Colburn, an American boy, a \"computing phenomenon\" that instantly multiplied six digits in his mind. Hamilton, self-confident and recited, called Colburn to an oral duel, and lost to an American, which was a psychological shock to a young genius.He realized that his arithmetic skills (as opposed to language) were not fast enough.\n\n♪ A student who became a professor before his diploma in 1827 was appointed professor of astronomy at the University of Dublin and a king's astronomer of Ireland, aged 22, and was not yet a university graduate and had no degree in master's or doctorate.Moreover, he has never worked as a practical astronomer or had any ability to handle the telescope! The University Council took an unprecedented step in breaking all the statutes because it saw \"the second Newton.\" The competitors resented it: \"The boy doesn't know the star sky!\" But Hamilton quickly calmed the critics... by proving himself right already in the area of optics, not star observation -- 4.\n\n♪ As Hamilton discovered... with a scandal, the story of conical refractation (1832) contains a funny domestic episode. Hamilton mathematically predicted that the light in the crystal of the aragonite would fall to the cone. His colleague Humphrey Lloyd decided to test it experimentally. But it wasn't that simple: the crystal was small and vague. Lloyd suffered for a week, saw nothing.And Hamilton wrote to Lloyd, \"Take a piece of aragonite that lies on your fireplace and serves as a press papier, and put it not side by side, but just as it lies on your side, diagonally.\" Lloyd followed this advice, and immediately saw the cone of light.Hamilton just remembered how he put the mineral on the table a month ago! The discovery was made not at the expense of sophisticated equipment, but at the expense of genius and attention to household matters. --5.\n\nThe most famous stone in mathematics, on October 16, 1843, Hamilton walked with Helen's wife along the Royal Canal in Dublin. Suddenly, he was struck by the idea of Quaternions. The fact: Fearing to forget the formula, he cut out the knife on the stone of the Broom Bridge (Brougham Bridge) the main identity: `i2 = j2 = k2 = ijk = -1'. Today, this bridge has a memorial sign on it. But there is a nuance: the bridge itself was rebuilt in the 1950s.The stone you saw in the old photo was replaced, but the Dullians honored the place: every year on 16 October, mathematicians from all over the world organized \"Hamilton Walk\" on this very side of the waterfront. And Hamilton himself was so proud of this event that he celebrated the anniversary of the discovery until the end of his life, writing in his diary: \"This is the day the Quaternions came to me.\" --6.\n\n♪ The war with flies and the noisy neighbors of Hamilton's marriage to Helen was so unhappy that the fights arose even because of... flies. Dancenc Observatory had a lot of damp and insects. Helen demanded that her husband buy mosquito nets on windows. Hamilton, loaded in quaternione calculations, refused in principle, claiming that \"the buzzing of a fly helps him to abstract from his own life.\" Helen responded by screaming that he was crazy and was leaving for family.Another fact: Hamilton was famous for playing piano at the observatory at 3-4 a.m., writing music (he was a good pianist). Neighborhoods (brothers from the nearest village) complained to the university that \"stars keep people awake\", but Hamilton ignored protests, saying that music helped him understand \"the symmetry of equations.\" --7.\n\n* A poet who forgot Hamilton's poems all his life considered himself a poet in the shower. When he was young, he even rotated in Dublin's literary circles and was friends with the poet William Wordsworth (by the way, Wardsworth was trying to talk him out of math, saying it was \"sucking his soul\".But the funny fact is that his poems were so complex and crowded with Greek mythology that none of the contemporarys, including the poet himself, could memorize them by heart. In public readings, he confused streaks and read one poem, and then came out another.\n\n♪ Drunk genius and a paradox of accuracy by the end of Hamilton's life ♪ ♪ He had been drinking heavily ♪ ♪ Neighbors and colleagues saw him often ♪ ♪ The paradox ♪ The night after the big whiskey ♪ ♪ Hamilton could sit in the morning with wild headache ♪ ♪ and write quateron equations with absolute mathematical accuracy ♪ ♪ without making mistakes in the signs (and I'll remind you, the order of multiplication is critical ♪ ♪ ij = k' and ji = -k' ♪His colleagues were checking the drafts and wondering, \"It was a man who couldn't get the key into the lockhole.\" He was slow to write, painfully checking every line. Alcohol seemed to remove his internal brakes and let his brain work at full speed. --9.\n\nIn 1846, Hamilton wrote a letter to his friend Augustus de Morgan (another great mathematician), predicting that the quaternions \"will soon become the main instrument for analysing physical space.\" But the interesting thing is, he wrote, \"the ideal application of the quaternions will be a description of the rotation of the sphere within the sphere.\"100 years later, this proved to be a precise description of the gyroscopes and the spaceship orientation systems! Hamilton couldn't know about space, but he just intuitionally saw a mechanic spinning in three-dimensional space. --10.\n\n♪ Hamilton's funeral irony died on September 2, 1865. His funeral in Dublin was attended by several hundred people, including the mayor and academics. Irony of the fact: Hamilton was a royal astronomer of Ireland for 38 years, but in his entire career he published only three astronomical observations (and they were not very accurate), and he was praised at the memorial as \"the greatest astronomer\", although everyone knew he hadn't looked at the telescope for decades.His scientific work was entirely theoretical, but the university didn't want to admit that for 38 years it was paid to a man who was doing pure mathematics, so the obituaries mentioned \"astronomy\" necessarily. -- 11.\n\n♪ The murder phrase about my wife in one of the letters to her friend, poet Aubrey de Vera, Hamilton wrote a phrase that became crystaltic: > \"If my wife loved math as much as I loved whiskey, we would be happy, but she likes whiskey as much as I love math, and that's why we're both unhappy.\" It was a bitter joke, but it's exactly the essence of his family life.Helen really, rumored to have been added to the bottle in times of depression, which only exacerbated the chaos in the house.\n\n~ Summary: Hamilton's antipod man was a walking contradiction: - Genius mathematician and a domestic loser. - Astronom, who hated the star sky. - A poet who wrote bad poems. - A sober in the mornings (forever for the past) and a drunken in the evening. - A husband who lived with one woman but loved another. All these strange things, however, do not diminish his greatness.His life is the best proof that genius and obsession often go hand in hand with human weaknesses."
